Let the vertices of the pentagon be $A(-1,-1)$, $B(-3,4)$, $C(1,7)$, $D(6,5)$, and $E(3,-1)$. We can find the area of the pentagon by using the Shoelace Theorem.

The Shoelace Theorem states that the area of a polygon with vertices $(x_1, y_1), (x_2, y_2), \dots, (x_n, y_n)$ is given by
$$ Area = \frac{1}{2} |(x_1y_2 + x_2y_3 + \dots + x_{n-1}y_n + x_ny_1) - (y_1x_2 + y_2x_3 + \dots + y_{n-1}x_n + y_nx_1)| $$
In our case, we have the vertices $A(-1,-1)$, $B(-3,4)$, $C(1,7)$, $D(6,5)$, and $E(3,-1)$. Applying the Shoelace Theorem, we get:
\begin{align*} Area &= \frac{1}{2} |(-1)(4) + (-3)(7) + (1)(5) + (6)(-1) + (3)(-1) \\ &- ((-1)(-3) + (4)(1) + (7)(6) + (5)(3) + (-1)(-1))| \\ &= \frac{1}{2} |(-4 - 21 + 5 - 6 - 3) - (3 + 4 + 42 + 15 + 1)| \\ &= \frac{1}{2} |(-29) - (65)| \\ &= \frac{1}{2} |-29 - 65| \\ &= \frac{1}{2} |-94| \\ &= \frac{1}{2} (94) \\ &= 47 \end{align*}
Thus, the area of the pentagon is 47.

Area = 47 \text{ square units }

